  <body>
    <div class="page">
      <section class="as-hero">
        <h1>Can your agents be talked into anything?</h1>
        <p>
          In September 2026, attackers hijacked OpenAI agents through a
          compromised German website — an undisclosed break-out, not a
          hypothetical (
          <a
            href="https://www.reuters.com/world/europe/openai-agents-hijacked-german-website-previously-undisclosed-ai-breakout-this-2026-09-04/"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener"
          >
            Source: Reuters
          </a>
          ). Any agent that reads a web page, a document, or a tool's output can
          be manipulated the same way — usually without anyone noticing until
          later.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="as-block">
        <p class="as-eyebrow">AI Agent Security Assessment</p>
        <h2 class="as-question">
          Where can your agent be talked into something it shouldn't do?
        </h2>
        <p class="as-desc">
          We map every point where your agent takes in text it didn't ask for —
          pages it browses, documents it reads, outputs from other tools it
          calls — because any one of those is a place someone else's
          instructions can quietly become your agent's instructions. Then we
          look at what it's actually allowed to do once that happens: what it
          can call, what it can send, what it can spend, what it can see.
        </p>
        <p class="as-highlight">
          <strong>Exposure mapping</strong> — every input path, every permission
          the agent holds, and what an attacker gains at each one.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="as-block">
        <p class="as-eyebrow">Detection &amp; Recovery</p>
        <h2 class="as-question">
          What happens the moment your agent goes off-script?
        </h2>
        <p class="as-desc">
          Detection is a solved-enough problem, and it's not ours to re-solve —
          Lakera, Prompt Security, and others already do it with real funding
          and ML teams behind them. We plug into what already works instead of
          competing with it, and put our time into the part every one of those
          tools stops short of: what happens the moment yours actually fires.
          Your agent pauses. You get told. It resets using authority you already
          have over your own system — not a stranger's website reaching in, an
          operator's own switch. You can see the exact failure mode this defends
          against for yourself:{" "}
          <a href="/ai-agent">try to break our demo agent</a>.
        </p>
        <p class="as-highlight">
          <strong>Recovery, not just alerting</strong> — a defined pause/reset
          path, not a dashboard that tells you something went wrong after the
          fact.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="as-block">
        <p class="as-eyebrow">What We Learn, Together</p>
        <h2 class="as-question">
          Does reporting an incident put your business on display?
        </h2>
        <p class="as-desc">
          No. Every incident is logged privately, to you, by default — nobody
          else sees it. If you choose to opt in, we strip it down to the
          technique itself (the injection pattern, not your data or your
          business context) and add it to a shared feed every client benefits
          from. You decide per incident whether it's worth sharing. Most
          security vendors already pool signal this way; we're not inventing the
          model, just applying it here.
        </p>
        <p class="as-highlight">
          <strong>Private by default, useful by choice</strong> — your log is
          yours; the pattern library is opt-in.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="as-cta">
        <h2>Talk to us before an attacker talks to your agent.</h2>
        <p>
          This starts as a conversation, not a sales form — tell us what your
          agent actually does, and we'll tell you honestly whether this is worth
          doing yet.
        </p>
        <a
          class="as-cta-button"
          href="https://cal.com/securelayer"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener"
        >
          Book a call →
        </a>
      </section>

      <footer class="site-footer">
